(since gon>39>2*\(14+3\). A degree-11 cusp would require 11|3.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(At 109, h=<23> has order 18 and fixes the degree-9 and degree-12) Tj T*
(supersingular points. The gonality bound exceeds 58, and hence) Tj T*
(2*\(14+5\) and 2*\(14+2\). The required 18|5 or 18|2 is impossible.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(At 113, the exceptional ordinary degree-8 orbit is fixed by h=<40>,) Tj T*
(of order 8. Since gon>63>2*\(14+6\), it would require 8|6.) Tj T*
(For a single degree-7 supersingular point use h=<2>, of order 14,) Tj T*
(which fixes the closed point. Again gon>63>2*\(14+7\), and 14|7 fails.) Tj T*
(All new stabilizers are checked directly on Tate coordinates.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Finally, two degree-7 supersingular points, or one degree-14 cusp,) Tj T*
(have C=0 and are fixed in reduction by h=<2>, of order 14. The) Tj T*
(bound gon>63>28 forces hD=D. The geometric h-orbit length is 14,) Tj T*
(or possibly 7 at j=1728. In the first case D is one orbit and maps) Tj T*
(to a noncuspidal rational point on X_0\(113\), contrary to Mazur's) Tj T*
(prime-isogeny theorem. In the shortened case the p-subgroup is) Tj T*
(stable under the CM automorphism. The underlying X_0\(p\) point) Tj T*
(has field Q\(i\), so the standard CM degree bound used in the proof) Tj T*
(of Derickx--Stoll Corollary 7.3 gives p-1<=2*[Q\(D\):Q]. But) Tj T*
(112>28. Thus this case is impossible as well. There is no shortening) Tj T*
(by 3, since 3 does not divide 14.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(These cases exhaust all reductions at 2 and complete the sixteen) Tj T*
(local exclusions. Together with Section 3 and the dyadic bound,) Tj T*
(they prove the theorem.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
